  was on a run of stanley cup wins number  13  walked away from the game there were too  many hits to the head  and he was diagnosed with traumatic  brain injury  i got to a point where i became hopeless  and very suicidal i was  extremely lost i had just cut off the  hockey community which is really the  only community that i knew  and i didn't know what to do next what  happened next was a trip to a mushroom  farm  where he learned about the compound that  makes the magic  psilocybin not only saved my life but  also helped me really reverse all of the  symptoms that i was suffering from today  carcillo doesn't recognize the man  he used to be the best healthiest  version of myself  turn on tune in  and drop out this is the version of  psychedelics  most people know synonymous with the  counter culture generation of the 1960s  but in the 50s in a small saskatchewan  city  on canada's prairies hallucinogenic  research was already underway  at the weyburn mental hospital  psilocybin  along with lsd was being used to treat  mental health issues  and alcoholism not only among patients  but among doctors and nurses too it was  here that the expression  psychedelics was first coined it's such  an interesting area of healthcare  because it does carry that cultural  baggage  dr evan wood is one of canada's leading  authorities on treating addiction  including how the war on drugs was a  setback cultural backlash  that led to essentially a total freeze  being put on research in this area  and really halting the science as well  so we're decades behind and we could  have been ahead of this  yeah it's a it's a sad state of affairs  and uh that's something that we probably  knew in the 1960s or 70s  so this is a small print that we took  from a mushroom that we grew in-house  this is the heart of research in canada  today  numinous a state of the art lab in  nanaimo bc  is growing millions of the rock star of  psychedelic mushrooms  cyloside cubensis lab workers are busy  extracting that crucial compound  psilocybin it's illegal to produce  possess  and sell psilocybin the active  ingredient in mushrooms  but numinus is the first company in  canada to have  legal permission to harvest this  hallucinogen  the company is about to begin clinical  trials on patients  so how do these mushrooms work on the  brain the brain has  all these pathways much like a highway  when a patient has a psychedelic  experience  different parts of the brain that  weren't connecting  now do this greater neuroplasticity  enhances the brain's ability to  reorganize thought patterns  which in turn helps those with  psychological disorders  to tap into different pathways for  dealing with their anxiety depression  pain or trauma and what psychedelics do  is they actually disrupt  the default mode network temporarily  taking it offline  in essence so that one can have an  experience that's different from being  trapped  in those same loops of thinking  dr devin christie wears two hats for  numinous medical doctor  and counselor she works with patients  who have chronic pain and trauma  a clinical treatment with psychedelics  can last up to eight hours  these medicines cause changes in our  brain  and in our perception that can be quite  profound  some examples are a sense of  timelessness or boundlessness  having mystical type experiences that  can be very spiritually significant or  meaningful  there can be visuals or  auditory senses or even something called  synesthesia when different senses blend  together in one's experience  outside a clinical setting it's clear  the future  is fungi canadians are consuming them in  pills  chocolate or tea but these products  contain  tiny amounts of magic mushrooms it's  called micro dosing  and this vancouver dispensary is seeing  a shroom  boom even if it's still technically  illegal  i don't know if you remember when  cannabis companies were first opening  and before they were fully regulated  we're kind of functioning in 